    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, what do you mean by a goblin? I meant like, what are the actual mechanical or environmental causes of a blockage?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I was just using the term \"goblin\" as a metaphor to illustrate the idea of a sneaky, hidden obstruction. In reality, a blockage in the pitot tube can be caused by a variety of mechanical or environmental factors.\n\nOne common cause is ice accumulation, which can form on the pitot tube during cold weather conditions. This can be particularly problematic in high-altitude or high-latitude flight regimes, where the air is colder and more prone to icing. The ice can block the pitot tube, causing the airspeed reading to become inaccurate.\n\nAnother cause is debris or dirt accumulation, which can occur due to exposure to the elements or poor maintenance. This can be particularly problematic in tropical or desert environments, where the air is dry and dusty.\n\nIn some cases, a blockage can also be caused by a malfunctioning or damaged pitot tube itself. This can be due to a manufacturing defect, wear and tear, or damage from a previous accident or incident.\n\nIt's worth noting that modern aircraft often have redundant pitot tubes or alternative airspeed measurement systems, which can help mitigate the effects of a blocked pitot tube. However, in some older aircraft or in certain flight regimes, a blocked pitot tube can be a",
